 - 5.1 ✅ Custom ticket type definitions validated: select needs options, options only on select, duplicate field ids refused, built-in field ids not shadowable, rank ≥ 1. (`packages/domain/test/ticket-types.test.ts`)
 - 5.2 ✅ Built-in ladder ships as seed data with ranks epic 1 / story 2 / task 3 / sub-task 4, materialisable against a workspace default workflow; `DEEPEST_RANK` is asserted against the shipped ladder's real maximum, so a deeper seed fails loudly instead of quietly releasing sub-tasks from the parent requirement. (`packages/domain/test/ticket-types.test.ts`)
 - 5.3 ✅ Every vocabulary event round-trips `parseEvent`; malformed payloads, unknown event types, and a ticket event filed against another kind of entity are all refused at the door. (`packages/domain/test/ticket-events.test.ts`)
 - 5.26 ✅ An `attachment.added` claim says which of the two kinds it is, or it is refused at the door: an upload round-trips with its `sha256`, a Drive reference round-trips with its pointer and `capturedAt`, and both a payload with no `kind` and an `upload` with no `sha256` are refused — an attachment whose kind is unstated is exactly the ambiguity these rules exist to remove. (`packages/domain/test/ticket-events.test.ts`)
 - 5.37 ✅ The server's `EngineContext` answers honestly about what it can serve while ticket state does not exist yet: `null` for a ticket and for a membership that HAS a head row, and the whole `{kind, id}` state for the kinds whose state is exactly that. A role-gated transition ingested end to end is refused as `unknown-ticket` — the true reason — and never as `unknown-workflow` naming a workflow called `undefined`, which is what the head-pointer cast produced for every transition. Coordination doc, Plan 1 consequences §9; every assertion fails against the body it replaced. (`apps/server/test/engine-context.test.ts`)
 - 5.38 ✅ A second `ticket.created` for a ticket that already exists is refused `ticket.already-exists`, ahead of every question about its payload. The reducer folds creation unconditionally (`packages/domain/src/ticket-state.ts:94`), so an accepted duplicate does not error and does not merge — it RESETS the fold, erasing the ticket's status, collaborators, field claims and the whole transition log four-eyes reads. Nothing refused it before, and the reset left no trace in head state. The order is pinned separately: a duplicate create whose type is ALSO undefined still names the duplicate, because the actor's type was never the problem. (`packages/rule-engine/test/hierarchy.test.ts`)
 - 5.39 ✅ Two refusals that used to fire with somebody else's sentence now tell the truth on the path that reaches them. A parent whose TYPE is undefined refuses `hierarchy.parent-type-unknown` naming the parent, not `ticket.unknown-type` — this ticket's own type resolved, so the borrowed copy sent the actor to define a type that is already defined (ruling P11). A parent in ANOTHER WORKSPACE refuses `hierarchy.unknown-parent`, not `hierarchy.cross-project`: ticket lookup carries no workspace predicate over a projection keyed `(entity_kind, entity_id)`, so a foreign parent resolves, and the more specific sentence was both false and a confirmation that the id exists somewhere the actor cannot see (ruling P12). Both are pinned as literals and mutation-proven, and the workspace case is staged against a parent that is foreign in project too, so the order is what the test constrains. (`packages/rule-engine/test/hierarchy.test.ts`)
